+### 2.2 References select node ID first; endpoint identity is resolved at dial time
+
+A reference to an existing node carries its globally unique `node.id`. An execution targeting an
+exact birth also carries incarnation. These fields stay structured. A lookup for a not-yet-created
+entry can carry separate `path` and `name` fields; the current pair claim resolves that composite
+index to a node ID. Once a node is admitted, later work retains its ID and exact birth as required,
+so renaming or replacing a node cannot redirect that work to another node.
+
+- A dial resolves node ID to the current endpoint from explicit topology metadata. Neither a node
+  ID nor a pair lookup stores an endpoint key.
+- Path is immutable for a node. Name changes leave its ID unchanged. A reference to the predecessor
+  remains a reference to that predecessor after replacement; selecting the successor requires its ID
+  or an explicit current-pair lookup. No reference silently follows a changed pair claim.
+- The node publishes the name its stored record holds. The node writes its own properties; its
+  mesh-primary writes lifecycle facts (§2.5).
+
+### 2.3 Replacement creates a new node ID at the original path and name
+
+A replacement has a fresh node ID. The predecessor keeps its own ID and path and changes only
+its name to `<name>-old`. The successor claims the original `(path, name)` pair. References to
+existing nodes continue to identify their original node IDs; a current-pair lookup selects the
+current claim explicitly.
 
